    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/bgsu/faculty/jobs/5527568/assistant-professor-paleobiology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Paleobiology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Paleobiology Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus (BG), OH Job Type Full-Time Faculty Job Number BGSU02064 Division A&S Dean Department School of Earth, Environ & Soc - Faculty Opening Date 10/23/2026 Description Benefits Summary The School of Earth, Environment, and Society (SEES) at Bowling Green State University (BGSU) invites applications for a full-time (9-month) tenure-track Assistant Professor with teaching and research specializations in Paleobiology, beginning in August 2027. Information about Program, College, and University As a Public University for the Public Good , Bowling Green State University is a comprehensive, high research university located in northwestern Ohio committed to driving economic and cultural vitality in the region and beyond. Through innovative teaching, research and creative activities, BGSU fosters a collaborative and inclusive learning community where inspired ideas, knowledge and achievements support the region, state and nation. More than 760 talented and passionate faculty members teach across more than 200 undergraduate majors and programs, 17 doctoral programs, and 57 master’s programs. A focus on promoting research, scholarship, and creative activities has resulted in a significant increase in extramural funding over the past several years. Guided by Forward , BGSU’s strategic plan, the University is focused on redefining student success. For the sixth year in a row, The Wall Street Journal ranks BGSU as the No. 1 public university in the Midwest that students would choose again. BGSU also holds the Wall Street Journal distinction of being the No. 1 public university in Ohio for the student experience. The School of Earth, Environment, and Society is one of eighteen units located within the College of Arts and Sciences. The vision for our College, aligned with the university’s strategic plan, is to ensure that our students graduate career-ready to meet the world’s challenges, and that our faculty research and creative activity contribute to the public good. We achieve this through robust collaboration across departmental boundaries, with particular attention given to increasing experiential learning for our undergraduate and graduate students and connecting our scholarly and creative work to the needs of our region. We are especially interested in recruiting candidates who can contribute to our strengths in teaching, scholarly/creative work, and/or service and toward achieving excellence throughout the university and the greater community. The College is committed to faculty success, providing resources and support within the Dean’s Office and through collaboration with Arts and Sciences departments and schools, leadership teams in other colleges, and university-serving partners such as the Center for Faculty Excellence. Through these connections, we have developed a comprehensive, cohort-based mentorship program to support the professional growth and advancement of faculty across all disciplines and ranks. We value and practice shared governance and are committed to maintaining a vibrant and supportive working environment. The School of Earth, Environment, and Society (SEES) offers both natural and social science courses and programs, including undergraduate degrees in Geology, Environmental Science, and Environmental Studies, and a Master’s degree in Geology. By bridging natural and social sciences, SEES prepares students for a variety of careers relevant to important societal issues, including water and energy resources, climate change and sustainability. Faculty in SEES have the opportunity to collaborate with the university’s Center for Great Lakes and Watershed Studies and assist with teaching the summer field geology course, currently taught in the American Southwest. For more information about the School, please visit the SEES website. SEES offers a Bachelor of Science degree in Geology, including the option of pursuing a Specialization in Paleobiology, as well as a Minor in Paleontology. The Paleobiology program has attracted talented undergraduate students from across the country for over half a century. SEES houses a collection of over 120,000 fossils with particular strength in the Paleozoic marine fossils of Ohio and the Great Lakes region, as well as extensive teaching collections to support courses in in invertebrate, vertebrate, and micropaleontology. Laboratory space and equipment for fossil preparation, photography, and microscopy are available in the school. Responsibilities of this faculty position include teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level, including general education and advanced courses in geosciences, Master’s student supervision, developing externally funded scholarship, and university service. The selected candidate is also expected to assist with the mentoring of undergraduate research, supported by an extensive endowment from the Don C. Steinker Paleobiology Endowed Fund. Essential Functions • Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in paleobiology and related areas of geosciences. • Advise undergraduate and graduate student research projects, including undergraduate Honors projects and Master’s student theses. • Conduct scholarly research in the area of paleobiology leading to publications in peerreviewed journals. • Attract external funding in support of the paleobiology research program. • Provide service to the school, college, university, profession, and community. Minimum Qualifications • Doctoral degree in the geosciences or related discipline by the date of appointment. • Primary research focus in paleobiology. Preferred Qualifications • Evidence of high-quality teaching in paleobiology and the geosciences. • Record of research activity and publication of paleobiology research in peer-reviewed journals. • Experience seeking or demonstrated success securing external grant funding in support of paleobiology research. • Evidence of effective integration of undergraduate research with the candidate’s research program. Additional Information Application Materials Required • Cover letter • Curriculum vitae • 1-2 page teaching statement • 1-2 page research statement • Names and contact information of three professional references Unofficial transcripts of highest degree earned are required for candidates invited for in-person interviews. Finalists will be required to submit official transcripts. BGSU policy requires finalists to authorize and pass a background check prior to receiving an offer of employment. Application Deadline The search committee will review applications until the position is filled; however, for full consideration, applications should be received by January 4, 2027. Salary and Benefits Competitive and commensurate with candidate’s experience and credentials. Full benefits package available. Further Information Questions should be directed to Dr. Margaret (Peg) M. Yacobucci ( mmyacob@bgsu.edu ), Professor of Geology and Paleobiology and Search Committee Chair, School of Earth, Environment, and Society, College of Arts and Sciences, Bowling Green State University, Bowling Green, Ohio 43403. Bowling Green State University is an equal opportunity employer. The University is committed to maintaining an employment and educational environment that is free from unlawful discrimination, harassment, and retaliation. The University prohibits discrimination and harassment on the basis of protected class: age (40 years of age or older), ancestry, color, disability, ethnicity, familial status, gender, gender identity and expression, genetic information, HIV/AIDS status, marital status, military status, national origin, political affiliation, pregnancy, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, protected veteran status, or any other legally protected characteristic. EEOC Know Your Rights OCRC Know Your Rights In compliance with the ADA Amendments Act (ADAAA), if you have a disability and would like to request an accommodation in order to apply for a position with Bowling Green State University, please call 419-372-8421 or email ohr@bgsu.edu . BGSU is an E-Verify employer. If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. A list of acceptable documents can be seen here: https://www.uscis.gov/i-9-central/form-i-9-acceptable-documents Benefits Bowling Green State University provides a comprehensive benefit program as part of a total compensation package. This includes medical, prescription, dental, vision, health accounts (medical & dependent), life & disability insurance, retirement plans, employee assistance program and tuition fee waivers for employees and their eligible dependents as well as paid time off, flexible work options, holidays and parental leave. For more information, please visit Benefits-at-a-Glance . Employer Bowling Green State University Address 1851 N Research Drive Huntington Building Bowling Green, Ohio, 43403 Phone (419) 372-8421 Human Resources (419) 372-2865 Student Employment Website https://www.bgsu.edu/human-resources/careers.html Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/bgsu/faculty/jobs/5521559/assistant-professor-photography": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Photography",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Photography Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus (BG), OH Job Type Full-Time Faculty Job Number BGSU02050 Division A&S Dean Department School of Art - Faculty Opening Date 10/19/2026 Closing Date 11/15/2026 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Summary The School of Art at Bowling Green State University seeks a dynamic teacher and artist to lead its Photography Area. Candidates should possess experience teaching traditional and digital photographic processes with secondary expertise in video and digital imaging. This is a full-time, tenure-track, nine-month appointment with a start date of August 9, 2027. Program and College Information Established in 1940, the School of Art supports traditional and emerging studio and scholarly practices across five academic divisions: Art History, Studio Art, Digital Arts, Graphic Design, and Art Education. The School offers a BA Art degree with specializations in Art History, Studio Art and Digital Art; BFA degrees in Studio Art, Graphic Design, Digital Arts and Art Education; MA degrees in Art History and Art Education (online); an MFA degree with specializations in Two-Dimensional Studies, Three-Dimensional Studies and Digital Arts; and a MDes degree in Integrative Design (hybrid/online). The RING Center for Experiential Learning, established two years ago through a major gift, has opened an exciting new chapter in the School of Art’s history. The RING Center provides funding to enable every School of Art student, regardless of their economic circumstances, to engage in significant experiential learning activities such as travel to conferences and workshops, study abroad, and creative and scholarly research. The School of Art is located within the College of Arts and Sciences. The vision for our College, aligned with the university’s strategic plan, is to ensure that our students graduate career-ready to meet the world’s challenges, and that our faculty research and creative activity contribute to the public good. We achieve this through robust collaboration across departmental boundaries, with particular attention given to increasing experiential learning for our undergraduate and graduate students and connecting our scholarly and creative work to the needs of our region. We are especially interested in recruiting candidates who can contribute excellence in teaching, scholarly/creative work, and/or service toward achieving excellence throughout the university and to the greater community. The College is committed to faculty success, providing resources and support within the Dean’s Office and through collaboration with Arts and Sciences departments and schools, leadership teams in other colleges, and university-serving partners such as the Center for Faculty Excellence. Through these connections, we have developed a comprehensive, cohort-based mentorship program to support the professional growth and advancement of faculty across all disciplines and ranks. We value and practice shared governance and are committed to maintaining a vibrant and supportive working environment. As a Public University for the Public Good , Bowling Green State University is a comprehensive, high research university located in northwestern Ohio committed to driving economic and cultural vitality in the region and beyond. Through innovative teaching, research and creative activities, BGSU fosters a collaborative and inclusive learning community where inspired ideas, knowledge and achievements support the region, state and nation. More than 760 talented and passionate faculty members teach across more than 200 undergraduate majors and programs, 17 doctoral programs, and 57 master’s programs. A focus on promoting research, scholarship, and creative activities has resulted in a significant increase in extramural funding over the past several years. Guided by Forward, BGSU’s strategic plan, the University is focused on redefining student success. For the fifth year in a row, The Wall Street Journal ranks BGSU as the No. 1 public university in the Midwest that students would choose again. BGSU also holds the Wall Street Journal distinction of being the No. 1 public university in Ohio for the student experience. Essential Functions In addition to formal teaching, faculty foster student success through academic advising, supervision of internships, and supervision of capstone and research projects. Tenure-track faculty must also maintain a creative research record commensurate with the School’s RPT guidelines and engage in School of Art, University and professional service. Minimum Qualifications • MFA or equivalent terminal degree in photography or related area (i.e. digital arts, video, new media). Applicants whose degrees are in progress may apply, but all the requirements for the degree must be satisfied before the position start date. • University-level experience teaching traditional and digital photography (as a Teaching Assistant, Instructor of record, or guest lecturer). • Documented record of professional achievement through exhibitions, residencies, and presentations. Preferred Qualifications • 2 years of university-level experience teaching photography (traditional and digital) courses. • Experience teaching video, and digital imaging courses. • Experience with narrative formats and digital illustration, as evident in professional portfolio. • Experience with interdisciplinary research and collaboration, as evident in professional portfolio. Additional Information Application Materials Required • Letter of Interest • Current Curriculum Vita • Teaching Statement centered on student success • 15-20 examples of student work (submitted as a URL or consolidated and organized PDF) • 15-20 examples of creative work (submitted as a URL or consolidated and organized PDF) • Names and Contact information of three (3) professional references. Unofficial transcripts of highest degree earned are required for candidates invited for in-person interviews. The search finalist will be required to submit official transcripts. BGSU policy requires finalists to authorize and pass a background check prior to receiving an offer of employment. Application Deadline November 15, 2026 Salary & Benefits Competitive and commensurate with candidate’s experience and credentials. Full benefits package available. Further Information Please direct questions about the application process to the Photography Search Committee Chair, John Balistreri ( balistr@bgsu.edu ) Bowling Green State University is an equal opportunity employer. The University is committed to maintaining an employment and educational environment that is free from unlawful discrimination, harassment, and retaliation. The University prohibits discrimination and harassment on the basis of protected class: age (40 years of age or older), ancestry, color, disability, ethnicity, familial status, gender, gender identity and expression, genetic information, HIV/AIDS status, marital status, military status, national origin, political affiliation, pregnancy, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, protected veteran status, or any other legally protected characteristic. EEOC Know Your Rights OCRC Know Your Rights In compliance with the ADA Amendments Act (ADAAA), if you have a disability and would like to request an accommodation in order to apply for a position with Bowling Green State University, please call 419-372-8421 or email ohr@bgsu.edu . BGSU is an E-Verify employer. If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. A list of acceptable documents can be seen here: https://www.uscis.gov/i-9-central/form-i-9-acceptable-documents Benefits Bowling Green State University provides a comprehensive benefit program as part of a total compensation package. This includes medical, prescription, dental, vision, health accounts (medical & dependent), life & disability insurance, retirement plans, employee assistance program and tuition fee waivers for employees and their eligible dependents as well as paid time off, flexible work options, holidays and parental leave. For more information, please visit Benefits-at-a-Glance . Employer Bowling Green State University Address 1851 N Research Drive Huntington Building Bowling Green, Ohio, 43403 Phone (419) 372-8421 Human Resources (419) 372-2865 Student Employment Website https://www.bgsu.edu/human-resources/careers.html Apply"
